.TH QEMATE 1 "October 9, 2026" "qemate 6.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
qemate \- Hardened QEMU virtual machine manager for Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qemate
.I COMMAND
[
.I NAME
] [
.I OPTIONS
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Qemate
is a streamlined command-line utility for managing QEMU virtual machines (VMs). It simplifies VM creation, control, and configuration, leveraging QEMU with KVM acceleration for enhanced performance when supported by the host system.

QEMU 11.0 or newer is required. Commands that create or resize images or launch a VM reject older QEMU tool versions. New VMs use the pinned \fBpc-q35-11.0\fR machine type; legacy \fBq35\fR configs execute with that same pinned machine ABI.

All commands are top-level with hyphenated names. There are no subcommand groups.

.SH COMMANDS
.SS VM Management
.TP
.B create NAME [OPTIONS]
Create a new VM with optimized defaults for Linux, Windows, or macOS.
.RS
.TP
.B --os-type TYPE
OS preset (\fBlinux\fR|\fBwindows\fR|\fBmacos\fR; default: linux).
.TP
.B --memory SIZE
Memory size (e.g., \fB4G\fR, \fB8G\fR, \fB2048M\fR). macOS defaults to 8G.
.TP
.B --cores N
Number of CPU cores (1\(en64).
.TP
.B --disk-size SIZE
Virtual disk size (e.g., \fB60G\fR). Defaults to 256G for macOS, 60G for Windows, and 40G for Linux. Creates a qcow2 image with metadata preallocation. Mutually exclusive with \fB--disk-dev\fR. Free space on the VM directory is checked before creation.
.TP
.B --disk-dev PATH
Raw physical block device to use instead of a qcow2 image (e.g., \fB/dev/sdb\fR). \fB--disk-size\fR is ignored when this is set. Resize and \fBclone\fR are not supported on physical-device VMs.
.TP
.B --machine TYPE[,accel=kvm|tcg]
Machine type (e.g., \fBpc-q35-11.0,accel=kvm\fR).
.TP
.B --enable-audio / --no-audio
Enable guest audio. Without \fBAUDIO_PASSTHROUGH_PCI\fR this attaches emulated Intel HDA using QEMU's default host backend (PipeWire, PulseAudio, or ALSA). Set \fBAUDIO_PASSTHROUGH_PCI\fR in the VM config for VFIO PCIe passthrough instead. Detached VFIO starts require passwordless sudo.
.TP
.B --enable-tpm / --no-tpm
Enable TPM 2.0 emulation (requires \fBswtpm\fR and \fBswtpm_setup\fR). Default: on for Windows, off for Linux.
.TP
.B --uefi / --no-uefi
Enable or disable UEFI firmware (default: on for Windows, off for Linux). Requires \fBedk2-ovmf\fR.
.TP
.B --spice / --no-spice
Enable SPICE display server (default port: 5930). Start prints a \fBremote-viewer spice://127.0.0.1:PORT\fR connection hint.
.TP
.B --share-backend auto|virtfs|smb|virtiofs
Select the shared folder backend.
.TP
.B --profile balanced|performance
Select the portable balanced policy (default) or opt in to preallocated, locked memory and tuned VirtIO queues. Linux and Windows VirtIO devices advertise negotiated packed/in-order rings, and multiqueue TAP networking advertises RSS. Hash reporting is enabled with QEMU userspace RSS but disabled with the vhost-net eBPF path, which does not support it. Performance mode maps block queues across up to four IOThreads. Hugepages are used when the pool has enough free pages (including with VirtIO-FS shared memory); otherwise qemate falls back to memfd or anonymous RAM. Creating with \fBperformance\fR validates available RAM and the process memlock limit. New performance-profile Windows VMs use VirtIO block and require its storage driver during installation.
.RE

.TP
.B start NAME [OPTIONS]
Start a VM. Log rotation runs automatically on each start.
.RS
.TP
.B --headless
Start without a graphical console.
.TP
.B --detach
Return after QEMU is ready and keep a detached qemate supervisor responsible for lifecycle cleanup. Independent of \fB--headless\fR, except when neither \fBDISPLAY\fR nor \fBWAYLAND_DISPLAY\fR is set: detached start then implies \fB--headless\fR so QEMU does not fail opening a GUI. The process cannot later be returned to the original shell with \fBfg\fR; use qemate status/stop and reconnect through a configured display service when needed. PCIe audio passthrough in this mode requires passwordless sudo (no TTY for interactive \fBsudo\fR).
.TP
.B --iso PATH
Attach a primary ISO image for installation.
.TP
.B --virtio-iso PATH
Attach a secondary ISO (e.g., VirtIO-Win drivers for Windows guests).
.TP
.B --debug
Print the full generated QEMU command to stderr before a foreground launch. For a detached launch, relay it to the caller before returning success or failure.
.TP
.B --dry-run
Print the shell-escaped effective QEMU command without binding host devices, creating TAP interfaces, or starting helper daemons. With \fB--detach\fR, also report the requested launch mode without spawning a supervisor.
.RE

.TP
.B stop NAME [--timeout SECONDS] [--force]
Request an orderly guest ACPI shutdown through QMP and wait up to 60 seconds by default. If the guest does not stop, it is left running. \fB--force\fR sends SIGKILL immediately.

.TP
.B restart NAME [OPTIONS]
Stop the VM if it is running, wait for lifecycle teardown, then start it again. Accepts the same options as \fBstop\fR (\fB--force\fR, \fB--timeout\fR) and \fBstart\fR (\fB--headless\fR, \fB--detach\fR, \fB--iso\fR, \fB--virtio-iso\fR, \fB--debug\fR, \fB--dry-run\fR).

.TP
.B wait NAME [--timeout SECONDS] [--state running|ready|stopped]
Block until the VM reaches the requested state. Default state is \fBrunning\fR/\fBready\fR (QEMU process live and QMP responsive). Useful after \fBstart --detach\fR. Default timeout is 120 seconds.

.TP
.B console NAME [--qmp]
Open an interactive QMP session on the VM Unix control socket (\fBsockets/qmp.sock\fR). The VM must be running. Requires OpenBSD netcat with Unix-socket support (\fBnc -U\fR).

.TP
.B logs NAME [-f|--follow] [-n|--lines N] [--qemate|--qemu|--supervisor]
Show recent log lines for the VM. Default source is \fBqemate.log\fR. \fB--qemu\fR selects the latest QEMU stderr log; \fB--supervisor\fR selects the latest detached-supervisor log. \fB-f\fR follows like \fBtail -f\fR. Default line count is 80.

.TP
.B clone SRC DST [--full]
Duplicate a stopped VM that uses a qcow2 disk image. Copies the config, assigns a new MAC address, and either:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
creates a \fBlinked\fR clone (default): a new qcow2 whose backing file is the source disk (fast, shares unchanged clusters). Deleting or shrinking the source disk breaks the clone; use \fB--full\fR for an independent copy, or
.IP \(bu 2
with \fB--full\fR: a standalone full copy of the disk.
.RE
.IP
UEFI NVRAM and TPM state are copied when present. VMs that use a host block device (\fBDISK_DEV\fR / \fB--disk-dev\fR) cannot be cloned: there is no private image file to duplicate, and pointing two VMs at the same physical disk is unsafe. Create a second VM with its own \fB--disk-dev\fR path instead.

.TP
.B delete NAME [--force]
Delete a VM and all its data. Prompts for confirmation unless \fB--force\fR is used. Locked VMs require \fB--force\fR.

.TP
.B resize NAME SIZE|+SIZE [--force]
Resize the VM disk image (e.g., \fB80G\fR or \fB+20G\fR). The VM must be stopped. \fB--force\fR is accepted for CLI compatibility but does not allow live qcow2 resizing. Not supported for physical-device VMs (\fB--disk-dev\fR). The config records exact GiB when the size is divisible by 1\ GiB; otherwise MiB.

.TP
.B list
List all VMs with seven columns: NAME, STATUS, LOCKED, OS, AUDIO, TPM, SPICE. Uses a lightweight config read (no full validation lock per row).

.TP
.B status NAME
Display detailed VM configuration and runtime status, including CPU, memory, disk, network, UEFI state, share backend, port forwards, USB passthrough, and shared folders.

.TP
.B configure NAME
.TQ
.B config NAME
Open the VM config file in \fB$EDITOR\fR, falling back to \fBnano\fR, \fBvim\fR, or \fBvi\fR. After editing, the config is validated against the qemate schema; if validation fails, the previous config is automatically restored. The VM may be running; changes then apply on the next start. The VM must not be locked.

.TP
.B tune NAME balanced|performance
Change only the runtime performance profile (\fBPERFORMANCE_PROFILE\fR) without replacing custom disk, network, passthrough, or shared-folder settings. Balanced Linux/Windows VirtIO devices advertise negotiated packed/in-order queues. Performance mode additionally maps block queues across up to four IOThreads. The VM must be stopped. For full multi-field edits, use \fBconfigure\fR.

.TP
.B doctor [NAME]
Report QEMU 11, host KVM, QMP client, AVIC, vhost-net, io_uring, and memlock readiness. With a VM name, also show the configured/effective machine type, VirtIO queue features and IOThread count, effective storage and shared-folder choices, and whether configured port forwards conflict with ports already bound on the host.

.SS macOS
.TP
.B macos-install NAME [--version VERSION] [--no-start] [--refresh]
Prepare an Apple recovery installer via kholia/OSX-KVM and start the VM by default. Uses a shared host OSX-KVM cache with a per-VM worktree (or local shared clone). Supported versions: high-sierra, mojave, catalina, big-sur, monterey, ventura, sonoma, sequoia, tahoe. Requires git, Python 3, and dmg2img. Use \fB--no-start\fR to prepare only.

.TP
.B macos-eject NAME
Detach recovery media after installation so subsequent boots use the installed disk through OpenCore. Also removes the temporary OSX-KVM checkout and recovery download artefacts, keeping permanent OpenCore and 4 MiB OVMF assets under \fBmacos/assets/\fR.

.TP
.B macos-recovery NAME
Re-attach recovery media for maintenance or reinstallation. Re-prepares media via \fBmacos-install\fR if it was cleaned up by eject.

.TP
.B macos-refresh NAME
Update the shared OSX-KVM cache and the VM's checkout (worktree or local clone).

.TP
.B macos-doctor NAME
Validate OpenCore/OVMF/recovery assets, the KVM \fBignore_msrs\fR requirement, and required QEMU macOS devices.

.SS macOS on a physical partition
To install macOS on a dedicated host partition, create the VM with \fB--disk-dev /dev/PARTITION\fR instead of \fB--disk-size\fR, then run \fBmacos-install\fR as usual. The partition must be dedicated, unmounted, and writable by the user running qemate; the macOS installer will erase it. Never select the host boot disk or a partition used by the host. Verify the target with \fBlsblk -o NAME,SIZE,TYPE,FSTYPE,MOUNTPOINTS\fR and \fBfindmnt -S /dev/PARTITION\fR before creating the VM.

For example:
.PP
.EX
qemate create mac-dev --os-type macos --disk-dev /dev/nvme0n1p6
qemate macos-install mac-dev --version sonoma --no-start
qemate macos-doctor mac-dev
qemate start mac-dev
.EE
.PP
In macOS Recovery, identify the target disk by the dedicated partition's expected size before erasing it as APFS; \fBMacHDD\fR is qemate's QEMU drive identifier, not a guaranteed Disk Utility label. If another displayed disk has the same size or the target cannot be identified with certainty, stop. After installation, stop the VM, run \fBmacos-eject mac-dev\fR, and start it again. Physical-disk VMs cannot be cloned or resized. Deleting the qemate VM removes its metadata but does not erase the host partition.

.SS Port Forwarding
.TP
.B port-add NAME [IP:]HOST:GUEST[:tcp|udp]
Add a port forwarding rule. Protocol defaults to \fBtcp\fR if omitted. The host port is checked for conflicts when the rule is added (and again at start).

.TP
.B port-remove NAME [IP:]HOST:GUEST[:tcp|udp]
Remove a port forwarding rule.

.SS USB Passthrough
.TP
.B usb-add NAME VENDOR:PRODUCT
Attach a host USB device by vendor:product ID (see \fBlsusb\fR(8)).

.TP
.B usb-remove NAME VENDOR:PRODUCT
Detach a host USB device.

.TP
.B usb-list NAME
List all USB devices configured for the VM.

.SS Shared Folders
.TP
.B share-add NAME [TAG:]PATH
Add a shared directory. The tag is auto-derived from the folder name if omitted.

.TP
.B share-remove NAME TAG
Remove a shared directory by its tag.

.TP
.B share-list NAME
List all configured shared folders for the VM.

.SS VM Protection
.TP
.B lock NAME
Lock a VM to prevent start, resize, configuration edits, and deletion (unless \fBdelete --force\fR). Stop and status still work.

.TP
.B unlock NAME
Unlock a VM.

.SH NETWORK BACKENDS
.TP
.B user
SLiRP userspace NAT. Default; no host setup required.
.TP
.B passt
Unprivileged backend using the host network stack directly (requires \fBpasst\fR).
.TP
.B tap
Kernel-accelerated TAP interface with optional host bridge. Requires \fBiproute2\fR and a pre-configured host bridge. Fastest option.
.TP
.B none
No network interface attached to the VM.

.SH SHARED FOLDER BACKENDS
.TP
.B auto
Picks the best available backend: virtiofs \(-> virtfs for Linux guests, smb for Windows and macOS guests.
.TP
.B virtfs
9P filesystem. Linux guests only; no extra host dependencies.
.TP
.B virtiofs
High-performance VirtIO-FS. Supports multiple simultaneous shared folders. Requires \fBvirtiofsd\fR. Windows guests also need WinFSP and the VirtIO-FS driver. The performance profile enables additional daemon cache/writeback options when supported.
.TP
.B smb
QEMU user-mode SMB share; limited to one folder. Requires \fBsmbd\fR from Samba on the host and NETWORK_TYPE=user or nat. Default for Windows and macOS when \fBauto\fR is selected.

.SH SECURITY & CONCURRENCY
Qemate implements strict \fBflock\fR-based locking on configuration files to prevent race conditions during simultaneous CLI commands. The script operates with a \fBumask 0077\fR to ensure all created files are accessible only by the owner. Config files are loaded via a strict regex-based lexer with key whitelisting — \fBsource\fR is never used, preventing arbitrary code execution from modified config files. Config file ownership and permissions (\fB600\fR) are verified and enforced on every load.

.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B QEMATE_VM_DIR
Overrides the default VM storage directory (\fB~/QVMs\fR).
.TP
.B QEMATE_OSX_KVM_CACHE
Shared OSX-KVM git cache directory (default: \fB$QEMATE_VM_DIR/.cache/OSX-KVM\fR).
.TP
.B QEMATE_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS
Age in days after which timestamped qemu/supervisor/virtiofsd logs are deleted on start (default: \fB14\fR).
.TP
.B QEMATE_LOG_MAX_FILES
Maximum number of timestamped qemu and supervisor log files kept per VM (default: \fB20\fR).
.TP
.B LOG_LEVEL
Sets logging verbosity (\fBDEBUG\fR, \fBINFO\fR, \fBWARNING\fR, \fBERROR\fR; default: INFO).
.TP
.B EDITOR
Editor used by the \fBconfigure\fR command (falls back to \fBnano\fR, \fBvim\fR, or \fBvi\fR).

.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/QVMs/VM_NAME/config
Hardened VM configuration file (permissions: 600).
.TP
.I ~/QVMs/VM_NAME/disk.qcow2
Primary QCOW2 disk image. Absent when \fB--disk-dev\fR is used.
.TP
.I ~/QVMs/.cache/OSX-KVM
Shared OSX-KVM repository cache used by macOS VMs.
.TP
.I ~/QVMs/VM_NAME/macos/assets/
Permanent OpenCore and 4 MiB OVMF assets retained after \fBmacos-eject\fR.
.TP
.I ~/QVMs/VM_NAME/logs/qemate.log
General execution log.
.TP
.I ~/QVMs/VM_NAME/logs/error.log
Symlink to the latest timestamped QEMU stderr log.
.TP
.I ~/QVMs/VM_NAME/logs/qemu-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.log
Timestamped stderr output from one QEMU run.
.TP
.I ~/QVMs/VM_NAME/logs/supervisor-YYYYMMDD-HHMMSS.log
Detached-start supervisor output.
.TP
.I ~/QVMs/VM_NAME/logs/virtiofsd_TAG.log
Per-share virtiofsd daemon log (one per shared folder when using the virtiofs backend).
.TP
.I ~/QVMs/VM_NAME/driver_state
Saved host PCI drivers for VFIO audio restore; retained if restore fails so a later attempt can finish.

.SH EXAMPLES
Create and start a Windows 11 VM with TPM and VirtIO drivers:
.EX
qemate create win11 --os-type windows --enable-tpm
qemate start win11 --iso win11.iso --virtio-iso virtio-win.iso
.EE

Create a macOS VM, prepare recovery media, install, then eject:
.EX
qemate create macvm --os-type macos
qemate macos-install macvm --version sonoma --no-start
qemate start macvm
# complete installation in the guest UI
qemate stop macvm
qemate macos-eject macvm
qemate start macvm
.EE

Create a VM using a physical disk:
.EX
qemate create rawvm --os-type linux --disk-dev /dev/sdb
.EE

Detached start, wait until ready, then inspect logs:
.EX
qemate start myvm --detach --headless
qemate wait myvm --timeout 60
qemate logs myvm --qemu -n 50
.EE

Clone a qcow2-based VM (linked, then full):
.EX
qemate clone myvm myvm-copy
qemate clone myvm myvm-full --full
.EE

Configure a VM (opens $EDITOR):
.EX
qemate configure myvm
.EE

Add a high-performance shared folder:
.EX
qemate share-add myvm /home/user/work
.EE

Forward a web port:
.EX
qemate port-add myvm 8080:80:tcp
.EE

Resize a stopped VM disk:
.EX
qemate stop myvm
qemate resize myvm +20G
.EE

.SH MACOS NOTES
Qemate supports x86_64 macOS guests using the OpenCore/recovery workflow from kholia/OSX-KVM. KVM acceleration and \fBignore_msrs=1\fR are required. After installation, install the guest-side mac-guest-agent (ISA serial) and optionally the VMHide Lilu plug-in; see the README for details.

